# Design flaws uncovered by the Brunerie measurement: Agda's evaluator, and ours

Companion to docs/the-brunerie-number-in-two-evaluators.md, which holds the
measurements in full; this report states what the measurements and the source show to be design
decisions with a measurable cost, each at the line that makes it and the count that prices it.

A word on register. Agda is a twenty-year type checker whose evaluator exists to serve checking, and
its authors have left their own marks on the places named below ("TODO", "fall back to slow
reduction", "a big chunk of the time during reduction is spent looking up definitions"). "Flaw" here
means a design decision whose cost this computation makes visible and countable, never a judgement of
the people who made it. Where this report says what Agda does, the line was read in this session in
the 2.9.0 source; where it says how often, the count is from Agda's own cost-centre profile of this
computation; where the cause is inferred rather than read, the sentence says so. The second half is
about our machine, by the same standard, because the investigation found as many flaws on our side.

