## 1. Agda

### 1.1 Two evaluators joined by a lossy seam

Agda reduces with two engines. `Agda/TypeChecking/Reduce.hs` lines 610 to 619 (`maybeFastReduceTerm`)
send a defined name or constructor applied to the fast engine and everything else to the slow one. The
fast engine, `Agda/TypeChecking/Reduce/Fast.hs`, is a call-by-need abstract machine: arguments are
pointers to thunks, so an argument is evaluated once however many times it is used. That is the right
design, and it is where Agda's sharing lives.

The machine handles the definitions it has a compact form for. `Fast.hs` lines 111 to 120 list the
forms: functions by compiled clauses, constructors, `primForce`, `primErase`, type constructors,
axioms, and `CPrimOp`, a primitive that runs on literals; the last constructor is `COther`, "in this
case we fall back to slow reduction". Lines 166 to 284 compute the form; the primitives given a
`CPrimOp` are those of natural numbers, characters, strings, names and metavariables (lines 270 to
282 end the list), and everything else is `COther` (line 284). The interval's connectives and the Kan
operations (`primTransp`, `primHComp`, `primComp`, the Glue primitives) are not in the list; as far as
the lines read, they are `COther`.

On meeting `COther` the machine does not stop computing; it leaves. `Fast.hs` line 1018: `COther ->
fallbackEval s`. `fallbackEval`, lines 1466 to 1478, "decode[s] the current focus to a Term, call[s]
slow reduction and pack[s] up the result in a value closure". `decodeClosure`, lines 816 to 826,
writes the environment and the spine out as a term by applying a parallel substitution to the body:
every shared thunk becomes a copy of its term at each of its occurrences. The slow engine then
reduces a tree, and the result re-enters the machine as a value.

The seam is where this computation lives. Per computation of `brunerie'` (§5.3a of the companion):

| what Agda did | times |
|---|---|
| entered the fast machine (`fastReduce'`) | 270,100 |
| fell back to the slow engine (`slowReduceTerm`) | 94,200 |
| decoded a closure to a term (`decodeClosure`) | 934,200 |
| performed a Kan operation (`primTransHComp`) | 24,500 |

Thirty-eight decodings per Kan operation; a fallback every third machine entry. The sharing the
machine built is written out as trees nearly a million times, at exactly the operations a cubical
computation is made of. The design decision is that the fast engine's instruction set stops at the
literal primitives; the cost is that cubical Agda's evaluator is, for cubical work, the slow engine
with a decoding step in front of it.

What would mend it, from the design: the Kan operations and the interval connectives as machine
instructions (a `CKan` beside `CPrimOp`), so that a composition's arguments stay thunks; or one
engine. The machine on our side has one evaluator: a Kan operation is a cell like any other, and its
arguments are shared through duplicators like any other's.

### 1.2 Names resolved at run time, inside the type-checking monad

Every step that needs a builtin or a definition asks the monad's state for it. `Agda/TypeChecking/
Reduce/Monad.hs` lines 27 to 30: `getBuiltinThing` does two `Map.lookup`s, one in the local builtins
and one in the imported, and unions the results. The fast engine knows this is expensive: its header
says "a big chunk of the time during reduction is spent looking up definitions in the signature", and
`memoQName` (lines 420 to 432) memoises the lookup in an `IORef`'d `Map` behind `unsafeDupablePerformIO`.
But the table lives inside one entry of the machine, and §1.1 counted 270,100 entries per computation.

| what Agda did, per computation | times |
|---|---|
| asked the monad for a builtin (`getBuiltinThing`) | 16,907,800 |
| computed a compact definition (`compactDef`) | 493,300 |
| entries in the name memo (`memoQName`) | 1,048,200 |

Forty-three builtin lookups per interaction of ours. In the sampled profile of the optimised build,
the monad's lookups are 10.5% of the computation (`HasBuiltins ReduceM` 4.4%, `BuiltinAccess` 2.1%,
`lookupCont` 1.8%, `defaultGetConstInfo` 0.4%). The design decision is evaluation as a monadic
program over the checker's state, which lets the evaluator see metavariables, options and the
signature as they change; the cost is that the identity of `primIMax` is a map lookup at every use
rather than a pointer fixed when the term was elaborated. Our machine fixes it: a compiled record
(`build_m`) points at the cells it uses, and a run does no lookup by name.

### 1.3 Substitution rebuilds the tree, and recomputes sorts on the way

`Agda/TypeChecking/Substitute.hs` lines 904 to 950, `applySubstTerm`: a substitution meeting a
variable looks it up; meeting a λ, an application, a constructor, a Π, it rebuilds the node with the
substitution pushed into every child. The comment dated 2026-03-17 states the intent: create no
thunks "until we hit the next inductive layer of terms", that is, eagerly one layer at a time. Every
face case of a composition substitutes a dimension by 0 or 1 through the term this way, and a term
of this computation is a large cube read at a few faces.

| what Agda did, per computation | times |
|---|---|
| applied a substitution to a term (`applySubst`) | 5,420,900 |
| traversed an argument wrapper (`traverse`, Agda.Syntax.Common) | 1,049,400 |

Fourteen substitution applications per interaction of ours. And the Π case computes a sort: lines
1499 to 1547, `mkPiSort a b = piSort (unEl <$> a) (getSort $ unDom a) (getSort <$> b)`, called from
the Π constructors used in substitution. In the sampled profile `mkPiSort` is 7.5% of the computation
(`mkPiSort_subE1` 4.6%, `mkPiSort31` 2.8%): a type's sort recomputed under every substitution that
passes a Π, for a computation that never asks for a sort.

The design decision is substitution as a function on trees, which is simple and correct. The cost is
work proportional to the term rather than to what is read. Our machine holds a substitution on a
reference and applies it one level as a value is read (`rule_subst`, `ref_read`, `subst_compose`); a
part never read is never substituted, and no sort is recomputed because no run asks the type of a
Π it merely passes through.

### 1.4 Terms without sharing nodes: the allocation bill

`Agda/Syntax/Internal.hs` lines 346 onward define `Term` as a tree: variable with eliminations, λ,
literal, defined name applied, constructor applied, Π, sort, level, metavariable. There is no node
that says "the same as that one". So the fast engine's sharing (thunks) is a property of the engine's
heap, not of the term, and is lost at every decoding (§1.1) and absent in every substitution (§1.3).

The bill, from GHC's runtime statistics (`+RTS -s`, profiles.log): loading `brunerie'` allocates
3,769,873,992 bytes; loading and computing it twenty times allocates 13,054,708,568; the difference
is 464 MB per computation of one integer. The collector takes 34% of the computation's samples
(`evacuate` alone 23.9%), and part of the 18% in the kernel is the heap growing. Memory management is
about 40% of Agda's time on this computation. Our machine's allocator is 20% of the reading, and a cell
freed by a rule is reused from a free list; a value nobody reads is erased level by level instead of
surviving to a collection.

### 1.5 Argument wrappers carrying metadata that every substitution resets

Each argument in Agda is `Arg { argInfo :: ArgInfo, unArg :: e }` with a derived `Traversable`
(`Agda/Syntax/Common.hs` lines 2903 to 2906), and `ArgInfo` carries, among else, a free-variable
annotation. `applySubstTerm`'s `subE` (Substitute.hs, lines 940 to 948, the reset at 944 to 945) resets that annotation to
"unknown" on every argument it substitutes under. In the profiled computing run the single largest
cost centre is this derived `traverse` at 8.2% of profiled ticks, entered 1,049,400 times per
computation. A profiled build distorts shares (the cost centres cost), so 8.2% is not a measurement of
the optimised binary; the count is exact. The design decision is metadata on every argument node; the
cost is a traversal and a reset per argument per substitution, for metadata this computation never
consults.

### 1.6 Face formulas decomposed from their syntax at each use

`Agda/TypeChecking/Primitive/Cubical/Base.hs` lines 354 to 391, `decomposeInterval'`: a conjunction
is the cartesian product of its parts' assignments, a disjunction their union, a negation flips them;
the result is a list of partial assignments with leftover neutral terms. Two "TODO"s on lines 360 and
361 (`primIMinDep`, `forall`) are the authors' own marks. The decomposition is computed from the
formula's syntax each time a system is matched, 108,900 times per computation, beside 564,900
`intervalView'`s (the view that reads a term as an interval connective, itself a builtin lookup each
time: `Agda/TypeChecking/Monad/Builtin.hs` lines 663 to 679, as the profile locates it).

The sampled profile prices the cubical primitives at 1.7% of the computation, so this is a flaw of
method rather than the gap's cause on these numbers. Our machine keeps faces as canonical De Morgan
normal forms (dnf.c) and compares them in place.

### 1.7 Loading dominates the user's time

Agda's own profiler on the computing module (`--profile=all`, profiles.log):

| phase | time |
|---|---|
| Total | 2,363 ms |
| Deserialization | 1,989 ms |
| Typing.CheckRHS (the computation) | 238 ms |
| Import | 93 ms |
| Parsing | 11 ms |

Eighty-four percent of a module's time is reading interface files. This is not an evaluator flaw and
the design (serialised interfaces, re-read per invocation) is a deliberate trade; it is listed because
anyone timing Agda from the shell measures this and not the computation, and because our machine
has the same shape in a milder form (§2.3).

### 1.8 The evaluator cannot be observed from outside

Agda's profiler attributes the normalisation to `Typing.CheckRHS` and prints no count of reductions,
Kan operations or face cases; the counts above needed a second Agda built with GHC profiling
(`-prof`, every dependency rebuilt with profiling libraries, one day of this work), and the time
profile of the optimised binary needed a difference of two perf recordings (compute twenty times,
subtract the load). Neither Agda nor our machine prints a measure of work common to both, so the
comparison of 395,608 interactions against 24,500 Kan operations is of two different units. A design
that exposed its evaluator's counters (`--profile=all` already has the hooks: `whenProfile
Profile.Conversion $ tick "compare"`, Conversion.hs line 158) would have made this report a day
shorter.

### 1.9 What the Agda findings add up to

| share of Agda's computation | what it is | counterpart in the machine |
|---|---|---|
| about 40% | allocation and collection (§1.4) | allocator 20% of the reading |
| 12% | GHC runtime: thunk updates, partial applications, maps | none |
| 10.5% | monad lookups (§1.2) | none; pointers in records |
| 10.6% | substitution, 7.5% of it sorts of Π (§1.3) | held substitutions and faces, 13% |
| about 9% | the evaluator proper: fast engine 4.3%, slow engine 3.2%, cubical primitives 1.7% | interactions 47% |

About a tenth of Agda's time on this computation is its evaluator evaluating. The rest is memory it
allocates and collects, names it looks up, sorts it recomputes, and trees it rebuilds. Each of those is
a design decision with a reason (a term that is a tree is easy to reason about; a monadic evaluator
sees the checker's state; a two-engine design let the fast engine be added without touching the
slow one), and each is priced above. The result is 0.236 s against the machine's 0.072 s, 3.2 times,
with 95% intervals that do not overlap (companion §3.1).

## 2. Our machine

The same investigation found design flaws of ours, two of them larger in their own terms than any of
Agda's.

### 2.1 An optimisation that was a pessimisation: the bound on pending operations (A8b)

The free-dimension cut (A8) stops a read where no dimension is free. Its bound (A8b) refused the cut
when the pending net below the port was too large, walking that net (fd_pending, fd_port, fd_cell,
box_fd) to decide. Thirty interleaved rounds by the step-counter clock (a8b.log):

| machine | reading's time | steps |
|---|---|---|
| A8 and A8b on (as it was) | 0.167 s | 358,691 |
| A8 on, A8b off | 0.070 s | 395,608 |
| A8 off | 0.088 s | 405,568 |

The bound saved 13% of steps and cost 2.4 times the time: the walk that decided whether to cut cost
more than the cut saved. The reading's time fell from 0.167 s to 0.070 s when it was switched off and
the comparison with Agda went from 1.5 times to 3.2 times. The design flaw is a general one: a bound
computed by inspection of the structure it bounds is a traversal, and a traversal is the cost the cut
was meant to avoid. The memory-is-local rule says it the other way: the cost of a process is derived
from its definition, never measured; had the bound's cost been derived, it would have been seen to be
of the order of what it saved. Avik: "Sure we can drop it or disable it by default". Switched off at
8dcef6d36, removed at ecf0dc6c9, recorded in lib/guard.cub.

### 2.2 Parallelism that slows the machine down: the worker threads, and why

An earlier version of this section measured four workers slower than one and proposed removal.
Avik, 2026-11-21: "You measured something slower on one workload and proposed deletion? Instead of
asking about cache layout, contention? The things that cause slowness with scaling?" The diagnosis
is in the handoff (docs/handoff-brunerie-measurement-session-2026-11-21.md §3.1); its results:

| Brunerie whole run, 5 rounds | 0 workers | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| elapsed | 2.78 s | 5.88 s | 6.71 s | 7.69 s | 7.99 s |
| processor time | 2.77 s | 8.56 s | 12.78 s | 17.87 s | 18.49 s |
| sched_yield calls | 0 | 3.1 M | 2.5 M | 2.9 M | 2.6 M |
| futex calls | 0 | 31 k | 83 k | 132 k | 150 k |

The loss is there with one extra thread and grows slowly after, three threads on four cores being
as slow as four: not contention growing with cores, not oversubscription. The width of the work,
measured on one thread with a scratch copy of the machine that histograms pending pairs at each pop:
on Brunerie 42% of pops find one pending pair, 85% find four or fewer, mean 3.4; on the library 47%
find one, mean 8.1 (258 M pops). At the granularity this executor distributes, one active pair of
about 87 ns, the computation under one observation is a chain. The mechanisms the code and counts
show: an idle thread polls the owner's queue lines at every steal attempt (51% of all samples in
`pop` with four workers), takes the chain's single next pair after a millisecond idle so the chain
and its cells migrate between cores, yields in a system call per idle iteration (3.1 M per Brunerie
run, 32 M per library run), and is woken and parked once per observation (1.4 to 1.7 M futex calls
on the library, whose readback observes once per node: the library's eleven times). Hardware
counters are not exposed on this machine, so coherence misses were not counted.

The design flaw is therefore not "threads" but the granularity, and the granularity has a derived
answer (Avik: "There is always a pure math answer as to the right way"), measured with the same
scratch copy: the steps' dependency graph has work W and depth D, no executor beats max(W/p, D), and
W/D is the available speedup. Inside observations, which run in sequence today, W/ΣD is 4.5 on
Brunerie and 3.9 on the library, and the Brunerie computation itself is a chain (W/D = 2.6); across
observations, which share no cell, the whole run's W/D is 57 and 32. The pair-level executor
distributes where the width is under 5 and the cost of a hand-off equals a step; the width is at
the observation level, where the cut is zero and the host's sequencing is what remains (the handoff
§3.1 has the derivation and the table).

### 2.3 The front end is expensive, and the remembered elaboration hides it

Removing the remembered elaboration (x_checked: the solved type and term of a declaration kept in the
store under the hash of its surface forms) was tried. Every value and verdict stayed identical, and
every warm run was five times slower: Brunerie 0.91 s with it against 2.69 s without, the library
4.42 s against 23.3 s, because each miss re-runs the elaboration's rounds, each of which runs the
checker to solve holes. The removal was reverted. Two flaws are visible here. The remembered
elaboration's key does not record what the elaboration observed (the signatures and instances it
depended on; the code's own comment says so), so a stale elaboration can be replayed, and it is sound
only because acceptance is never taken from it: §10.1 of the paper asks for a certificate, and this is
not one. And the front end it hides costs 2.7 s on a computation whose evaluation is 0.07 s: like
Agda's 2 s of deserialisation (§1.7), the user's time is the load, not the computation, in a ratio of
about forty to one.

### 2.4 Measuring a switch that was gone: the memo series

A hundred-pair series on 2026-11-20 compared the machine with its memo table on against off and found
no difference. The memo and its keys had been removed from main two days earlier (0bace3c2e, by
another session); the series compared one machine with itself. The flaw was in the investigation's
control: a switch's existence was taken from a record (the workflow node said the memo was present)
and not from the current net.c. The twelve-pair series of 2026-11-17 at e14aaba1, the last machine
with the memo, stands: switched off, 13% more steps on the reading and no difference in whole-run
time. The rule that came out of it: "Never answer anything about any code without reading that exact
line of code" (Avik, 2026-11-18), applied to measurement.

### 2.5 The pool's index is rebuilt without a lock

The source is a pool of entries read through an index that `index_sync` (syntax.c lines 2350 to
2433) rebuilds whenever the stamp of the entries differs from the stamp recorded: it clears an
`index-next` directory, reads every entry, writes the new index beside the old, then renames each
file over the old one and writes the stamp. Nothing in those lines excludes a second process. On
2026-11-21 a leftover four-worker run of the library (from the series of §2.2) and a `./hyperactive`
command rebuilt the index together; the result was an index in which lib/check.cub could not be
opened ("a file loaded without the checker"), repaired by killing the stray process and removing the
stamp so that one process rebuilt cleanly (live 497, modules 476, alternatives 0). The design flaw is a
rebuild in place that is correct for one process and undefined for two.

### 2.6 Merging two pools makes alternatives

Merging main's pool into this session's converted 24 stale name pointers into root entries
(`replaced=-`) beside main's own entries for the same paths (commit c77b0972f). The pool's rule is that
two live entries at a path are alternatives, which merge while they bind equal text and block every
read of the path the moment one side moves; the standard library moved first, and the path was
unreadable until the extra entry was stated dead. All 24 are now dead with the reason recorded. The
flaw is that a merge of two pools has no notion of "the same entry arrived twice"; the fix in design
terms is an entry identity that survives the merge, or a rule that a root entry binding the text
another live entry already binds is dead on arrival.

## 3. Flaws of method in this investigation, and their corrections

These were the previous session's and mine, and Avik caught each one.

| flaw | how it was corrected |
|---|---|
| single measurements reported as numbers ("obviously don't just do one measurement") | every number from repeated, interleaved runs with mean, sd and se; 100 rounds where cheap, 30 where not; the rule "A number is never one measurement" in the welcome file |
| the comparison was against Agda 2.6.3, three years old ("you used a 3 year old version") | 2.9.0 and 2.8.0.2 built from Hackage; the rule "A comparison is against the current release of the other system" |
| the reading's time was inferred from step counts and stated as a measurement | the step-counter clock (progress lines timestamped under strace) measures it directly; checked against perf's realtime window |
| perf attributed by stacks through `readback_at`, which dwarf unwinding truncated | the window is taken from the realtime clock, not from stacks |
| "whose belief" was left ambiguous | every corrected belief named as this session's or the previous one's |
| tables put the two systems' numbers apart | the same thing on both systems adjacent |

## 4. What this report does not claim

Not that Agda's design is wrong for Agda: a type checker's evaluator must see metavariables and
options, and trees are what a checker reasons about; the flaws above are costs of those choices on
this computation. Not that our machine is well designed where it was not measured: §2 is what the
investigation found, not an audit. Not that the gap is explained: §1.9's shares add to how Agda spends
its time, and the companion's §6 says which causes are known and which are conjecture. And not that
the comparison is more than one computation on one machine, against one other system, at one date.

## 5. Open

- Agda side: a count of the terms decoded at each fallback (sizes, not entries) would turn §1.1's
  conjecture of lost sharing into a measurement; a `CKan` experiment in `Fast.hs` would price it.
- Our side: the worker threads' diagnosis leaves the granularity question (handoff §3.1); the remembered elaboration's key is to be
  completed into a certificate (paper §10.1); `index_sync` needs exclusion or an atomic rename of the
  whole index; the pool needs an entry identity across merges.
- Both: a unit of work both systems print, so that a comparison is of counts and not only of seconds.
